WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.815
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:09.594
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:17.167
Unit eleven: Advanced Modal Perfect. Today you will use modals with perfect infinitives for past deduction and regret.

4
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:20.212
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.701
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:26.307
Can't have.

7
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:30.535
Can't have means a strong deduction that something did not happen.

8
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.360
She can't have left.

9
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.684
Your turn. Say: Can't have.

10
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:39.430
Can't have.

11
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.277
Needn't have.

12
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:45.444
Needn't have means an action that was unnecessary but done.

13
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.388
I needn't have hurried.

14
00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:50.787
Your turn. Say: Needn't have.

15
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:54.785
Needn't have.

16
00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:56.423
Would have.

17
00:00:57.417 --> 00:00:59.997
Would have means a hypothetical past result.

18
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:01.747
I would have helped.

19
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:05.026
Your turn. Say: Would have.

20
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:08.752
Would have.

21
00:01:09.517 --> 00:01:11.574
Modal + perfect infinitive.

22
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:16.591
Modal + perfect infinitive means modal + have + participle.

23
00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:18.393
Must have gone.

24
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:22.623
Your turn. Say: Modal + perfect infinitive.

25
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:28.650
Modal + perfect infinitive.

26
00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:30.685
Past certainty.

27
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:34.786
Past certainty means a firm conclusion about the past.

28
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:36.605
Past certainty.

29
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:40.055
Your turn. Say: Past certainty.

30
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.702
Past certainty.

31
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.391
Unnecessary past action.

32
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:52.577
Unnecessary past action means something done but not needed.

33
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.554
An unnecessary past action.

34
00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.149
Your turn. Say: Unnecessary past action.

35
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.941
Unnecessary past action.

36
00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:07.439
Hypothetical past result.

37
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.320
Hypothetical past result means what might have happened.

38
00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:13.928
A hypothetical past result.

39
00:02:14.517 --> 00:02:18.226
Your turn. Say: Hypothetical past result.

40
00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:22.904
Hypothetical past result.

41
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.953
Speculation.

42
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.099
Speculation means guessing about unknown facts.

43
00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:31.799
Speculation about the cause.

44
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:35.686
Your turn. Say: Speculation.

45
00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:39.825
Speculation.

46
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:44.168
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:48.305
Visal, the lights were off, so can you say they were not at home?

48
00:02:49.417 --> 00:02:59.012
They can't have been at home, or They must have gone out. Must have shows strong certainty and can't have shows strong certainty that something did not happen.

49
00:03:00.117 --> 00:03:01.733
What about weaker guesses?

50
00:03:02.817 --> 00:03:13.364
May have, might have and could have show possibility: He might have missed the bus. Should have and ought to have criticise a past failure: You should have called.

51
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:15.799
And needn't have?

52
00:03:16.883 --> 00:03:26.757
Needn't have means the action happened but was not necessary: I needn't have taken an umbrella. Compare didn't need to, where we do not know whether it happened.

53
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.389
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:34.850
Grammar focus: Modal perfect: modal plus have plus past participle.

55
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.442
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.685
She can't have left already.

57
00:03:39.583 --> 00:03:41.397
I needn't have taken an umbrella.

58
00:03:42.283 --> 00:03:43.853
He might have missed the bus.

59
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:53.681
Must / can't have = past deduction. May / might / could have = possibility. Needn't have = unnecessary action. Should have = past criticism.

60
00:03:54.417 --> 00:03:57.917
Be careful! Don't say: She must left already.

61
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:02.226
Say: She must have left already.

62
00:04:02.526 --> 00:04:04.886
Use modal + have + participle.

63
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.283
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:10.335
The Missing Keys.

65
00:04:10.635 --> 00:04:12.774
The lights were off, so they must have gone out.

66
00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:16.323
She can't have taken the keys, because she was abroad.

67
00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:19.066
He might have left them in the car.

68
00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:21.939
I needn't have searched the whole house.

69
00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:24.921
We should have checked the car first.

70
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:30.128
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:30.983 --> 00:04:33.169
She blank left; her coat is here.

72
00:04:33.469 --> 00:04:36.692
A, can't have. B, mustn't. C, needn't.

73
00:04:40.233 --> 00:04:42.419
The answer is A: can't have.

74
00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:46.712
I blank taken an umbrella. It stayed sunny.

75
00:04:47.012 --> 00:04:50.793
A, needn't have. B, can't have. C, must have.

76
00:04:54.333 --> 00:04:56.379
The answer is A: needn't have.

77
00:04:57.517 --> 00:04:59.284
He might have blank the bus.

78
00:04:59.584 --> 00:05:02.471
A, miss. B, missed. C, missing.

79
00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:07.708
The answer is B: missed.

80
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.281
Should have means blank.

81
00:05:10.581 --> 00:05:14.606
A, past criticism. B, future plan. C, habit.

82
00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:21.258
The answer is A: past criticism.

83
00:05:22.417 --> 00:05:24.753
According to the reading, where might the keys be?

84
00:05:25.053 --> 00:05:29.020
A, in the car. B, in the garden. C, in the bag.

85
00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:34.299
The answer is A: in the car.

86
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.791
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:43.728
Write a sentence using 'needn't have' about an unnecessary past action.

88
00:05:52.517 --> 00:05:53.982
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:54.282 --> 00:06:06.016
I needn't have bought so much food for the party, because only half of the guests came. I did buy it, but it was not necessary. Needn't have describes that kind of wasted effort.

90
00:06:06.316 --> 00:06:07.874
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:08.783 --> 00:06:14.930
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal perfect: modal plus have plus past participle.

92
00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:19.264
Today's value is reflection. Think about what you learned and how you grew.

93
00:06:19.564 --> 00:06:22.367
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:25.622
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

